nt art association reached out  to us they had the same problems  everyone who's teaching right now  we need to do this remotely we want to  support the robust  student art community at mit how do we  do this when we're all distributed over  the summer  so we worked closely with them and we  developed this six-week pilot program it  was kind of unglamorous and  involved packing up boxes with art  materials shipping them to people  all around the country and then meeting  once a week for a few hours to discuss  and critique one another's creative work  um the goal the goal of ramp for us  in addition to doing some meaningful  teaching was that it gave us  the opportunity to pilot a few prototype  ideas that we have  and get user feedback from both  undergraduate and graduate students at  mit  so we specifically we tested three  pieces of curriculum that we hope to  develop  over the coming 12 months those are  going to become products from live  release  the first was curriculum about color  material and  pigment formulation people are taking  objects from the world  turning them into paint the second was a  kit that dealt with cyanotype and  photography without the camera  and the third was a curriculum that  looked at rule-based and generative  drawing systems  yes and currently we're working on  building a pre-launch prototype  which will be made available to a  limited audience this  winter so it's one of the kits that  michael mentioned it's the cyanotype and  photographic  experiment kit and it explores the  like the historical photographic  technique of cyanotype  which allows students to produce  photographs without a camera  using only and sunlight and this kit  will include light-sensitive cyanotype  sheets  as well as a series of design challenges  and historical reference  and a range of materials that will help  the students explore and experiments  with  with this technique that sounds  fantastic  i have to throw this shameless plug if  this sounds compelling to you or you  have a young person  in your family or in your kind of orbit  we hope to get these available  before the holidays so we'll put the  link in the chat if you sign up with  your email we'll let you know  just as soon as we're finished working  on it excellent  i'm also i neglected to introduce my  fantastic  co-host his name is theo fields i  introduced him in the beginning of the  last session but i know that we've had  some new people join us  so theo is my co-moderator and is also  going to be asking some questions to  everybody  yes thanks for the uh the warm intro um  some questions for  ellen aka the spacex team here  um so you mentioned having uh outposts  in new york city  and also kind of a move to pivot your  business model a little bit away from  indoor spaces more towards outdoor  spaces  safe collective gathering who have been  your kind of key partners in that and  what have been  the steps that have been made that  successful and viable  oh i think you are on youtube  ah yes classic um thanks for the  thoughtful question  um i think you know creative reframe  earlier mentioned that nobody does  this type of work alone and that is very  much true in our case there's a  constellation of  of partnerships and resources we rely on  every day  i mean um not the least of which are  folks that you mentor  or that you meet rather through  organizations and competitions like this  truly we are still in touch with mentors  that we met through this very  competition  um we email them constantly for  big big asks and little asks um  and um so there's mentors that we keep  in touch with who  who provide us with resources and  insight there  are folks in the city that have been  particularly essential for us you know  working in the real world  um you know in physical space often  requires us to think about things like  permitting licensing how high can a  banner be  et cetera et cetera um and we've found a  lot of great resources even um just  right in boston  um i suppose i'll give like a quick  shout out to a group called the mayor's  office of new urban mechanics  a division of the mayor's office that is  specifically focused on sort of r  d in the city of boston perhaps  applicable to many teams  these are folks who are really  interested in testing and experimenting  in the city of boston and best  of all they know exactly who to put you  in touch with if there's a specific  um division of the city that might be  applicable to what you're working on so  they've been a huge resource for us and  then i think lastly and maybe most  importantly  our customers um you know we work with  landlords and we work with artists  and talking to them constantly has  provided us with the kind of  um invaluable insight about what our  next step should be you know it's  stephanie and i working full time at  spaceus but we could  go around in circles for hours you know  um  validating one one move or the other and  then you know questioning ourselves but  the clarity that we find when we just  pick up the phone and talk to somebody  who's known us from the beginning  who we've worked with from the beginning  and who is you know out there in the  world  experiencing a reality that is unique to  them and unique to them as a customer of  ours  that has been illuminating so whether  it's through surveys or just picking up  the phone and talking to people  are you comfortable going inside of  spaces yet no like what does that mean  what would you be comfortable with  um and just having a um a landscape of  folks that you can call  and get their feedback on has been  essential for us as we've like both  continued to  operate in boston and look elsewhere as  well  fantastic i think there's a great  takeaway for students which is uh real  customer research  right real customer conversations is  crucial  um speaking of students in the  competition um near and john you guys  have both mentioned  uh mentored students in this competition  before  um we were curious how kind of fully  developed are some of the ideas when you  first encounter them or when you first  encounter the student teams and what  is the most impactful in helping them  kind of refine  reform those ideas as they go through  the competition  john john you want to go first you want  me to go  i can uh why don't you go ahead first  i'll go right after okay  great um so i'll tell you something i  mean  we keep mentioning it but i think one of  the most important thing is the  the team and i always say that i prefer  an a team with a big idea  than a b team with an a idea because the  atm will know how to turn around  an idea and the nature of startup is  that you change on a constant basis so  you have to be agile and flexible  changing and other things that  i kind of what i try to do at least with  the teams that i  mentor is that to really push them into  the validation  phase often we have and a startup is  basically  a bunch of assumptions that you need to  validate and i always encourage them  to leave the building what they say  don't don't stick to the sovereign i  think and just mention it  having a meaningful conversations with  with a customer potential customer is  super  super important in order to validate a  problem  because at the end i tell them you don't  want to find yourself in  sys what we call solution in search of a  problem  you want to really make sure that not  only that you have idea that you have an  idea for the problem that you actually  discover and i think that the teams that  actually go outside of the comfort zone  and  do outside go outside from the building  and do  speaking to customers those are the ones  that actually able to  make the progress in the a competition  um and yeah and what i again what i try  to do as a mentor is  not to provide an answer but rather  asking questions to kind of  guide them through questions and  challenge what they say or challenge  what they think  and one of the exercises that i ask my  teams to do  is to actually think about everything  that can go wrong with your startup  and try to think how are you going to  tackle it because in the world of  startup everything that can go  wrong most likely will go wrong so  that's kind of my  perspective  that was fantastic  um i can give i can give a little i'll  give a little short  thing as well um i think one of the most  important things when you guys are  coming up with these  ideas is is like to share it and share  it with a lot of people there's  we we get really caught up with holding  ideas really close to our hearts and  being afraid that if we share it with  someone they might take it from us so  they might  do it quicker but the reality is is  there's probably dozens  um if not hundreds of people that are  coming to the same conclusions that  you're coming to  at the same time and the only way  to sort of win this race is to do it as  a relay with a team  and to be really really confident about  that team but to not  hold things in so so test with as many  people as possible talk to people about  it  um it's still going to be you know your  idea and your baby  you know try to solve a common problem a  simple problem not a super complex one  especially for this competition  because you're just gonna it's gonna be  very difficult you're gonna run out of  time and you want something that  is simple but iterative that you can  really iterate on  um think about you know areas that give  people anxieties how you can solve those  sort of things  that that kind of stuff is becoming  really really important right now in  in the environment that we're finding  ourselves in so i think you guys should  really focus  on that and then you know don't  necessarily focus on something that  you're  good at obviously you should do  something an area that you're good at  but it's  it's too easy to be sort of uh to get  tunnel vision to be caught up in this  like  great idea about something that you  already know a lot about and something  that you're already good at  but generally that's not always going to  end up being  the best idea that you may be able to  come up with sometimes  the idea you least expected will come up  so you have to be willing to  have a lot of ideas be willing to you  know throw them out quickly and iterate  and sort of let the process guide you  um like like an ocean swell would guide  you you know control your ship  um set a destination but know that that  the pathway there isn't going to  necessarily be exactly what you expected  um so yeah avoid tunnel vision at all  costs and uh that's that's what i have  to say about that yeah these are all  fantastic insights this is great advice  um in the spirit of keeping things fresh  and lively  and you know again not succumbing to  zoom fatigue  i want to take a few minutes now and do  a full group sort of lightning round  um so we'll do two minutes roughly each  and i'd like you all to tell us what is  one key  piece of advice or sort of pearl of  wisdom that you would give to  prospective competitors in this  competition let's go with elements  okay i can start um so for me the one  key piece of advice  would be forming a team that shares the  same vision and the same values as you  do  i think the team defines the culture of  the startup and that's why working with  people whose beliefs and whose values  aligning to yours  are is so important to make your  adventures successful  and i mean successful not only in terms  of launching and  raising capital but also successful at a  personal level  i think um meaning that uh something  that is meaningful to you  and that you believe is adding value to  society  so i think that would be oh and someone  also someone who's  um who you enjoy working with because  you're going to spend a lot of time with  them  i think that would be mine  yeah absolutely i obviously i agree with  all that  um my advice again we're very early in  on 