lt to overcome  but you've got to you know get out of  that comfort zone in order to thrive and  flourish in the way that you're hoping  to succeed  um so ellen same question  yeah i think if i was talking to myself  two years ago just starting out on this  i would remind myself that um  no matter the outcome of the competition  um  you're going to learn more about  yourself than maybe even you learn about  your venture in this process  um and that that is  a worthy venture if you will um i  i also think that it's an incredible  opportunity  to get outside of the walls of mit a  place that can often be  very claustrophobic and  inward looking we used it as an  opportunity to  to finally get to know neighbors that we  never interacted with when we  uh you know spent all of our time in  building seven in studios  um and so i would use it as an  opportunity  to get to know yourself better as a  human as somebody who's going to be  joining  the workforce as somebody who works with  others  no matter what happens on stage it is  it is um an incredible opportunity to  get to know yourself a little bit better  too  i really appreciate that answer because  we're really trying to facilitate  edification learning betterment for  everybody at all stages and  it you know it shouldn't be end game  driven i think the  the focus and the priority which is  becoming such a rich part of the mit  entrepreneurship  ecosystem is that there's a spirit of  you know inquisitiveness and learning  and that you know  regardless of who gets the great big  check at the end of the day  ultimately there should be value added  and mentorship for everybody  so um yes absolutely uh nier  your turn uh i'll use some of the things  that you mentioned i mean for me  the the check at the end is not what's  important entrepreneurship  it's it's a mindset and it's a way of  living so whether if you will succeed  this competition or no  it's tools that will serve you later on  in life and the other let's say that you  need to start at least nine companies  until you  succeed with the 10th so it's a long  it's a long game  entrepreneurship um having said that  it's super important to  to use speed you need to make as many  iteration as you can with a  very limited time that you have and  creatives i really love to  to work with creative there is one a  challenge often is that they are so  passionate about what they are doing  they are so focused on the product the  presentation of the service that they  forget there is a customer outside  so it's important to have a great a  product but make sure that you speak to  your customers and when you go to your  customers  don't search for the answer you want to  hear come open-minded be  active observer in order to be able to  really understand  what they are looking for sometimes they  don't know what they want until you show  it to them so  that's my takeaway  i really like that the the insights that  everybody seems to be sharing today  center around network partnerships  willingness to fail  willingness to learn so john do you have  any other follow-ups for that  yeah one of the things i'll say is like  um always keep  a really tight pulse on like  socio-economic political situations and  i mean in this case i think that  for myself and a lot of entrepreneurs  that i know kovid19  quickly had us pivot but we were in a  situation where we were kind of already  able to do that and some of the  technology projects that we were working  on for sort of two years out from now  we ended up being able to look at those  technologies and kind of like  use them find uses for them now where  they could provide  um assistance and help um in the  education realm and in some various  other  areas so yeah having a very strong pulse  on  sort of the adoption of technology  obviously is really important  but but especially just you know what's  going on in the world where are the  anxieties where are the problems that  can be solved and trying to find that  problem that  is kind of relatively new and doesn't  have solutions yet for  it but we can imagine will be in place  for at least the next  next decade um that's that's what i  would really try to focus on  i think it's very important what you  just said things like  ongoing viability and sustainability it  touches back on what ellen said about  thinking about transcending the bounds  of mit  when you're a member of you know the big  bad real world  you still want your venture to sustain  and thrive and  having that mindset that forward  thinking mindset i think is essential  toward ongoing  success toward forging deeper  partnerships getting more buy-in  and building your longevity so we've got  about five minutes left in the panel  if anybody has any other conversational  points or questions or tidbits or things  that you feel like offering please let  me know i  don't necessarily want to end early  because we have a break i would love to  keep the pace going and i know that we  have so many good ideas and  great brains in this room right now  oh it looks like we have a question from  ceta um  go for it hey folks thanks so much for  taking the time to  share your experiences it's so  inspirational and so energizing so i  love it  um my question is um as someone who who  has  done some venture stuff related um uh  things in the past but not not quite in  the arts space  i'm curious to understand your  perspectives on what what have you found  are  um unique barriers and roadblocks in  building  a startup in the art space um and how  did you or how did you advise your  mentees to overcome those barriers  it's for us the mentos  uh for for both mentors and and uh  founders i mean i'll tell you generally  i think that when you come to a new  space there  are no experts in the new by definition  if it's new  nobody can have experience in that so  don't let the fact that you are not  art history graduate or not an artist by  yourself  to stop you from